; deny_capability.sb
;
; Seatbelt (sandbox-exec) profile used by capability_scope.sh to run a single subprocess (and
; everything it forks/execs) with the destructive-filesystem-write CAPABILITY categorically
; removed, while leaving everything else about that subprocess's environment alone --
; already-open file descriptors (stdout/stderr inherited from the invoking shell), network,
; process exec, reads, all still work normally, per (allow default) below. Only the ability to
; open/create/write/delete a filesystem path is removed.
;
; WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (PRD.md R13/R14a, TESSERA DEVH-61 through DEVH-64,
; PRD-DELTA-R14-R15.md): a command-string guard like bollard/guard_destructive.py matches on the
; TEXT of a command and can in principle be evaded by an obfuscation its pattern list has not
; seen yet -- the same su""do-class evasion problem PRD.md R13 names. Capability removal at the
; OS level does not depend on recognizing the command's text at all: once a process tree is
; denied the file-write capability, no shell-level trick executed INSIDE that tree can restore
; it, because the kernel enforces the denial on every open/unlink/rename syscall regardless of
; how the invoking command string was assembled.
;
; WHY A BLANKET DENY, NOT A SCOPED SUBPATH (unlike deny_keychain.sb, which scopes to Keychain
; paths specifically): this profile's declared capability is "destructive filesystem writes" in
; general, not writes to one named resource. `guard_os_sandbox.py`'s own capability-class check
; (which commands get wrapped in this profile at all) is what keeps the blast radius bounded --
; see that file's CAPABILITY_CLASS_PATTERNS, reused from guard_destructive.py's own
; DESTRUCTIVE_PATTERNS rather than a second, competing matcher. This profile itself denies the
; capability unconditionally for whatever process it wraps; it does not re-implement the
; command-shape decision that already lives in guard_os_sandbox.py.
;
; WHY ORDINARY COMMAND OUTPUT STILL WORKS UNDER THIS PROFILE: Seatbelt's file-write* operations
; gate NEW filesystem path lookups (open/create/unlink/rename), not writes to a file descriptor
; the process already held open before sandbox-exec applied the profile. A wrapped command's
; inherited stdout/stderr therefore keeps working -- this is what makes the POSITIVE case
; (C14a: a command that never opens a new path for writing) distinguishable from the TRAP case
; (C14b: a command that does), rather than every wrapped command failing identically regardless
; of what it does.
;
; EMPIRICAL VERIFICATION: see bollard/test_capability_scope.py (C14) for the three-case
; positive/trap/negative-control run against this exact file via the real capability_scope.sh,
; the same discipline deny_keychain.sb's own header documents and DEVH-63/DEVH-64 both
; independently reproduced against a scratch version of this profile before it was shipped here.

(version 1)
(allow default)

(deny file-write*)
